{% doc %}
  Arms the motion prestage, and disarms it if the motion runtime never boots.

  Entrance animations start from a hidden state, and that hiding has to happen
  before first paint or the content flashes. The obvious way to do that — a CSS
  rule that hides every un-animated element — has a failure mode with no
  recovery: if the runtime 404s, throws, or simply never arrives, the content
  stays hidden forever and nothing notices.

  So the prestage rules in critical-css are keyed on `data-motion-armed`, and
  this script owns that attribute:

    - it sets it, so a page that never runs this script hides nothing;
    - it removes it again if the runtime never arrives, so a failed load
      degrades to "no animation" rather than "no content".

  That second check is no longer a 2s timer. The timer was a guess racing a
  normal page load, and on a slow connection the load won: measured on Slow 3G,
  motion-component.js finished at 7.3s, the watchdog had disarmed at 2s, and all
  8 elements on the page stayed un-animated while the runtime sat there fully
  loaded — plus a state nothing reconciles, booted=true with armed=false.

  Three triggers replace it, all calling the same flag-gated check, first one to
  fire wins:

    - a `error` on one of the motion scripts. A script that failed to load is a
      definitive answer AT THAT MOMENT, so a 404 or a blocked request recovers
      in milliseconds instead of waiting out the page.
    - `window.load`. NOT DOMContentLoaded: the obvious reading is that deferred
      module scripts always run before DCL, but this repo already carries a
      workaround saying otherwise — see the "Safari 15: Module scripts may load
      after DOMContentLoaded has fired" comment in src/js/modules/page-transition.js.
      Settling on DCL there would disarm the prestage while the runtime was
      still on its way, which is no animation at all on that browser, on every
      load, however fast the connection.
    - a 20s backstop, because `load` has no upper bound either. A single hung
      request — not failed, just never answered — means neither of the events
      above ever fires, and the content stays hidden for as long as the network
      stack takes to give up. That is the exact bug this mechanism exists to
      prevent, arriving from the other direction. 20s is chosen to sit clear of
      the 16.6s DOMContentLoaded measured on Slow 3G above, so a genuinely slow
      page is never mistaken for a broken one.

  Each half of the runtime reports for ITSELF, because each gates a different
  prestage rule and they can fail independently:

    - `data-motion-booted` (motion-component.js) gates the single-element
      prestage;
    - `data-motion-groups-booted` (motion-group.js) gates the list prestage,
      together with `data-motion-builtins`;
    - `data-motion-text-booted` (motion-text.js) gates the word-by-word
      heading prestage, `data-motion-text-armed`.

  One flag for both would hide the case this pairing exists for: motion-group.js
  failing on its own left every list prestaged and invisible forever, while
  motion-component.js reporting in told the watchdog everything was fine.

  `data-motion-builtins` is separate again and set below by probing, because
  `customElements.define` with `extends` can be unsupported even when the module
  loads perfectly — and that probe has to happen before first paint.

  Must render in <head> BEFORE critical-css.

  @example
  {% render 'motion-boot' %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- if settings.animations_enabled -%}
  <script>
    (function () {
      var root = document.documentElement;
      root.setAttribute('data-motion-armed', '');
      root.setAttribute('data-motion-text-armed', '');

      /* Customized built-in support, tested by trying — there is no capability
         flag for it. This has to happen HERE rather than when motion-group
         loads: a prestage that only applies after the module arrives is a
         prestage that arrives after first paint, which is the flash it exists
         to prevent. */
      try {
        var probe = 'motion-probe-' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2);
        customElements.define(probe, class extends HTMLDivElement {}, { extends: 'div' });
        root.setAttribute('data-motion-builtins', '');
      } catch (e) {}

      /* Idempotent and gated on the flags, so every trigger below is safe to
         fire, in any order, more than once. */
      function settle() {
        if (!root.hasAttribute('data-motion-booted')) root.removeAttribute('data-motion-armed');
        if (!root.hasAttribute('data-motion-groups-booted')) root.removeAttribute('data-motion-builtins');
        if (!root.hasAttribute('data-motion-text-booted')) root.removeAttribute('data-motion-text-armed');
      }

      /* A script that errored is a definitive answer right now. Capture phase:
         `error` on a script element does not bubble. */
      document.addEventListener(
        'error',
        function (event) {
          var target = event.target;
          if (target && target.tagName === 'SCRIPT' && /motion-(component|group|text)\./.test(target.src || '')) settle();
        },
        true
      );

      if (document.readyState === 'complete') settle();
      else window.addEventListener('load', settle, { once: true });

      setTimeout(settle, 20000);
    })();
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
